</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-lim-simplify">
<title>Evaluating a Limit by Simplifying</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)}
\frac{\frac{x^2}{2} - xy + \frac{y^2}{2}}{x^2 - y^2}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Direct substitution gives <m>0/0</m> again, but this time the expression
factors: pull <m>\tfrac12</m> out of the numerator to reveal a perfect
square, and factor the denominator as a difference of squares. One factor
then cancels <mdash/> say why that is legitimate as
<m>(x,y) \to (1,1)</m> <mdash/> and what remains submits to direct
substitution.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Factoring:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {Why the cancellation is legitimate:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {The limit:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for factoring the quotient, for justifying the cancellation, and for the resulting limit.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-lim-continuity">
<title>Continuity</title>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-continuity-two-variables">
<title>Continuity at a Point and on the Domain</title>
<statement>
<p>
A function <m>f(x,y)</m> is <term>continuous at the point</term>
<m>(x_0,y_0)</m> if
</p>
<p>
<alert>1.</alert> <m>f</m> is defined at <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>,
</p>
<p>
<alert>2.</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} f(x,y)</m> exists,
</p>
<p>
<alert>3.</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
<p>
A function is <term>continuous</term> if it is continuous at every point
of its domain.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cont-2xy">
<title>Continuity Everywhere Except at the Origin</title>
<statement>
<p>
Show that the function
<md>
f(x,y) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{2xy}{x^2+y^2} \amp (x,y) \neq (0,0)\\
0 \amp (x,y) = (0,0)
\end{cases}
</md>
is continuous everywhere except at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Two separate arguments. Away from the origin, say what kind of function
this is and which rule of <xref ref="thm-skel-lim-properties"/> then
settles continuity in one step. At the origin, the work is
<xref ref="ex-skel-lim-dne"/> again with a factor of <m>2</m>; give the two
paths and the value along each, and name the condition of
<xref ref="def-skel-continuity-two-variables"/> that fails.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.5) -- (15,8.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {Away from the origin:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.2) {At the origin, two paths:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Condition that fails:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for continuity away from the origin, for the two paths at the origin, and for the condition of the definition that fails.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-cont-xy2">
<title>A Discontinuity Detected by a Curved Path</title>
<statement>
<p>
Show that the function
<md>
f(x,y) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{xy^2}{x^2+y^4} \amp (x,y) \neq (0,0)\\
0 \amp (x,y) = (0,0)
\end{cases}
</md>
is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Start with the whole family of straight lines <m>y = mx</m> at once,
keeping <m>m</m> as a letter; simplify <m>f(x,mx)</m> and take
<m>x \to 0</m>. You should get the same answer for every <m>m</m> <mdash/>
which is exactly why this example is here, so say what it does
<em>not</em> prove. Then look at the degrees in the denominator to see
which curved path would make the two terms balance, and try it.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {Along $y=mx$: $f(x,mx) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {What this does not prove:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {A curved path that balances the denominator:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Conclusion:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the function along the family of straight lines, for what that computation fails to prove, for a curved path that gives a different value, and for the conclusion.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-lim-parabola-path">
<caption>
Every straight line through the origin gives the limit <m>0</m>, but
along the parabola <m>x = y^2</m> the function is constantly
<m>\frac12</m>. A limit must agree along <em>all</em> paths, not merely
all straight ones.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-lim-parabola">
<diagram dimensions="(380,320)" margins="12">
<definition>par(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.85,2.6,1.85)">
<axes at="pp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<line at="pp-l1" endpoints="((-1.25,-0.42),(1.25,0.42))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l2" endpoints="((-1.25,-1.0),(1.25,1.0))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l3" endpoints="((-1.25,0.42),(1.25,-0.42))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l4" endpoints="((-1.25,1.0),(1.25,-1.0))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l5" endpoints="((-1.25,1.6),(1.25,-1.6))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l6" endpoints="((-1.25,-1.6),(1.25,1.6))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="pp-par" function="par" domain="(-1.35,1.35)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<point at="pp-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>
<label at="pp-par-lab" anchor="(1.9,1.32)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>x=y^2:\; f=\tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="pp-lines-lab" anchor="(-1.35,-1.55)" alignment="sw" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>y=mx:\; f\to 0</m>
</label>
<label at="pp-x" anchor="(2.45,-0.04)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="pp-y" anchor="(-0.04,1.72)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate plane with six grey straight lines of different slopes all passing through the origin, and one thick orange parabola opening to the right, also passing through the origin. The grey lines are labelled f tends to zero and the parabola is labelled f equals one half.">
  <annotation ref="pp-l2" text="One of the straight paths y equals m x through the origin. Along every such line the function tends to zero."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-par" text="The parabola x equals y squared. Along this curved path the function is constantly one half, which is why the limit fails to exist even though every straight line gives zero."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-O" text="The origin, where the function is defined to be zero."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
There is a second route to the same conclusion. Give it. (Once you know
the value along the curved path, you do not need a second path at all:
compare that value with <m>f(0,0)</m> and use condition <alert>3</alert>
of <xref ref="def-skel-continuity-two-variables"/> directly.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the alternative argument that the function is discontinuous at the origin.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
